// The boss journal: Garrow, the Moon's Hound, first hunter of the Silver Road, in each hero's own words (docs/plans/journal-voices.md)
// Garrow never speaks (he does not even bark), so there are no lines from him. Move keys are cast names (the journal fires on the cast bar's name).
// The Long Mere keeps every step anyone takes on it: a pale talon over a head means a hunt on that hero's own footprints (On the Scent), and every page
// that follows is a way of asking the same question: where have I walked? Mist-Hounds walk one hero's steps each; two that meet go out together.
// The Long Chase is the Whiteout (three hunts in a row from clean snow); The Whole Trail is Act IV, when the snow stops and the prints stop fading.
// Cold Trail is the enrage and has its own page, as the other bosses' enrages do.
// Double Scent, Doubling Back and Buried Scent are the Hard-only pages (hard: true). Crossed Trails (Hard) is a rule with no cast of its own: it is folded into
// those three pages (a fresher track crossing his road turns him onto it), and so is Blood on the Snow (Extreme: a hero caught by something avoidable leaves red
// prints, and at a crossing he takes red first), which lives inside The Second Hound and Stillness.
// The Second Hound and Stillness are the Extreme-only pages (extreme: true): each is a hunt cast under the rule's name.
(window.JOURNAL_DATA = window.JOURNAL_DATA || {}).garrow = {
  // the first time this hero meets him (after the first pull)
  meet: {
    bastion: "A wolf the size of a house came out onto the frozen lake at night, with a broken silver chain on him and two small moons for eyes. He did not bark. The snow kept every print I made, and the only warm thing in sight was a row of windows far across the ice.",
    gloomsworn: "A hound as big as a barn, silver bones under a skin of mist, and never a sound out of him. I looked back at my own prints, one long dark line to the shore. I have left such lines before. Far across the ice somebody had lit windows, and not for me.",
    thresher: "Such a quiet gentleman, dear, and so very large, with two little moons for eyes and a broken silver chain. He came down the Moon's road ahead of her household and never once barked. The snow keeps every step we take, which I find rather charming.",
    bloodreaver: "The big one came out of the mist with a snapped silver chain trailing and two small moons for eyes, and he never snarled once! I do not trust a quiet beast, and I loved him at once. The snow kept every print I left.",
    runeguard: "The Moon's Hound, the old lore says, comes down her road ahead of her household, and the lake at night keeps every step laid on it as a clerk keeps a ledger. He does not bark. Two small moons for eyes, a broken silver chain, and lit windows far across the ice.",
    gorgemaw: "A hound the size of a feast hall, frost-mist over silver bone, and my host did not so much as growl. Across the lake, a row of lit windows, the only warm thing in the room. I have eaten at colder tables, and seldom a quieter one.",
    oathbroker: "The Moon's Hound has come down her road ahead of her household, as the old agreements provide, bearing a broken silver chain. He does not bark. The snow upon the lake enters every step taken on it, and there is no clause for erasure.",
    ironpalm: "He did not bark. A hound the size of a house stood on the frozen lake and looked at us with two small moons. The snow kept every step. Far across the ice, a row of lit windows, and nothing else warm anywhere.",
    ranger: "Big wolf. Frost over silver bone, two pale moons for eyes, broken chain. No bark, no growl. Fresh snow on the lake keeps every track, mine included. Far shore had lit windows. Nothing else warm for miles.",
    shadowblade: "The mark is a wolf the size of a house and he never makes a sound, which I find terribly refined. The snow on the lake keeps every step, so for once even I leave a signature. Lit windows far across the ice, and no invitation.",
    pyromancer: "A hound the size of a barn, made of frost, with two small moons for eyes, and he never barked once! The lake was the coldest stage I have ever stood on. Far across the ice, a row of lit windows, the only warm colour in the whole picture. I resented it.",
    thermist: "Cold clean through, a wolf of frost-mist over silver bone, and not a sound out of him. The snow kept every step I took. Lit windows far across the lake: warm, small, out of reach. I ran cold all evening.",
    driftblade: "He came out of the mist like a storm that had forgotten how to blow, big as a house, silver chain trailing, no bark at all, and the snow on the lake kept every step I took across it, and I took a great many.",
    alembist: "Specimen: one hound, house-sized, frost-mist over silver bone, two small moons for eyes. Silent. Note: the fresh snow on the lake records every step. Label: lit windows on the far shore, warm, small, and a long way off.",
    threadweaver: "A great grey hound the size of a house, with a broken chain hanging off him and not a sound out of him. The snow on the lake kept every stitch of ours, every print. Far across the ice there were lit windows, which I took for a promise.",
    marionettist: "A new set, and a vast one: a frozen lake at night, a row of lit windows far across it, and our star arriving silent as a held breath, big as the house, two small moons for eyes. (The snow kept every step the company took. The Doll left prints too.)",
    cleric: "Poor creature, so large and so silent, with a broken silver chain still hanging on him and two small moons for eyes. He has come down the Moon's road ahead of her household. The snow on the lake keeps every step, and the lights across the ice looked very far away.",
    grovekeeper: "An old wolf out of the weather, big as a barn, mist and silver bone, never a sound from him. The snow lay fresh on the lake and kept every step we made in it, the way a field keeps a plough's track. Lit windows far off, past the ice.",
    chronomancer: "Garrow comes down the Moon's road a little ahead of her household, and I rather think he arrives before he has set out. He does not bark. The snow on the lake keeps every step, so this time, which was also last time, I looked back at mine.",
    tidecaller: "A hound like a drift of sea fog, vast and silent, with two small moons for eyes and a broken silver chain. The lake was black water under snow, and the snow kept every step. Far off, a row of lit windows, and I hummed toward them.",
    lanternkeeper: "A long night with a hound's shape, house-large, and not a bark out of him. The snow on the lake held every step I made, and across the ice a row of lit windows, the only lamps in the world. I counted them. A few were low.",
    kenseier: "A hound out of the mist, vast and quiet, silver bone and a broken chain. He does not bark, and the snow on the lake keeps every step as ink keeps a stroke. A teacher who gives no warning but the lowering of his head.",
    houndmaster: "Big grey lad on the lake, house-sized, frost over silver bone, a snapped chain on him. Never barked. Rust and Ash went stiff-legged and quiet beside me. Fresh snow keeps every print, theirs and mine. The dogs knew him for what he was.",
  },
  // the first time this hero beats him
  win: {
    bastion: "He lay down on the ice, the chain fell off him, and the mist sank into the snow. Our prints were all still there to walk home on. Nobody was left out on the lake.",
    gloomsworn: "The mist drew off him and he was only silver bones, and then nothing. I looked back across the lake at our prints, a long dark road, and at the windows past the ice, and thought some of us had earned that warmth.",
    thresher: "Down he lay in the snow, dear, with the chain at last gone and his two small moons going out. A very polite ending. The road is clear now for the household behind him.",
    bloodreaver: "The wolf went down, and I counted my scars by the light of the far windows: a good many, and all earned fair. A fine hunt, my friend, and a quiet one.",
    runeguard: "The Moon's Hound is down, and the whole hunt is set in the margin: every line walked, every landing, every sign over a head. The snow kept our steps and I have kept them in ink. Her household may come down the road now.",
    gorgemaw: "A cold, silent feast, and I cleared every plate. The mist sank into the snow like gravy into bread, and the lit windows across the ice looked suddenly like an invitation. I intend to accept.",
    oathbroker: "The hunt is concluded and the Moon's Hound released from it, though the broken chain, I observe, stayed broken. Our footprints remain upon the snow as the only record. I have declined to sign them.",
    ironpalm: "The hound lay down. No bark, no last word. The mist sank into the snow and the snow was only snow again. We stood on the ice with our own prints behind us and the windows ahead.",
    ranger: "Down. Wolf's gone to mist and the mist to snow. Our tracks run back across the lake and none of them cross. Lit windows ahead. Walked toward them.",
    shadowblade: "At the end the mark let the dark have him, on the open lake, which was courteous of him. For once I left a trail the whole way across, and I walked it home without a glance behind me.",
    pyromancer: "He went down and the whole lake went quiet, and the windows across the ice looked as warm as a theatre! I will be loud about this for weeks. The only thing brighter than the moon that night was my mood.",
    thermist: "The lake is perfectly still now, and I am exactly as warm as the windows. The snow kept every step I took, forward and back. Balance, for once. Wrote it down with a frozen pen.",
    driftblade: "When he sank, the lake opened up like a field, and I ran it end to end, round the ridges and back, leaving a trail a mile wide and not caring who read it. The windows were closer by the end.",
    alembist: "Logged: one hound, resolved into mist, then into snow. Samples: none, he left none. Notebook: every footprint of mine is in it, in order. Label for the shelf: do not follow your own steps twice.",
    threadweaver: "The great grey dog is gone, and I counted every stitch of mine, and each one's prints led back to me. The windows across the ice are real; I saw somebody moving behind one. Come along, all of you.",
    marionettist: "Curtain on the Long Mere. Our star lay down in the snow, chain and all, and the lit windows across the ice stayed lit for the company. (The Doll took the first bow. Her footprints were the neatest.)",
    cleric: "He lay down at last, poor creature, and the mist went into the snow like a sigh. Everyone I had was alive on the ice, and every one of them had walked there on their own two feet. I gave thanks for the windows.",
    grovekeeper: "The old wolf is down, and the snow will bury him by morning, and the lake will freeze deep over our tracks like a field under frost. All my lot walked off it. Sit down now, and take a cup toward those windows.",
    chronomancer: "He lay down, and I did not have to undo a single step of the evening, which is a first. The snow kept our tracks, and I looked at them a long time. Every one of them went forward.",
    tidecaller: "The hound went out like a fog that has said all it has to say. The black water slept under the snow, and our footprints were the only sound on it. I sang a little, toward the windows.",
    lanternkeeper: "He lay down in the snow. I lit one lantern on the ice where his chain fell and left it burning for the household behind him. Four of us walked toward the windows along four lines, and none crossed.",
    kenseier: "The hound is down, and the lake is calm. Every hunt had its beat, and the last was his own. He came without a sound, and I bowed to him in the snow. A courteous opponent, and a silent one.",
    houndmaster: "Big grey lad's gone to mist and the lake's quiet. Rust and Ash came out of it wagging, wet to the belly. Followed our own prints back across the ice toward the windows. Supper at last.",
  },
  moves: {
    'On the Scent': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "The sign went to one of the others, never to me. I kept my ground and kept the line off the walker's prints. When the hound came back out of the mist he stood where he had stood before. Steady.",
      gloomsworn: "A pale talon settled over one of us. That one walked a long straight line, far from the rest, and stepped aside at the end of it. I kept to my post and off their prints. I have walked longer roads than that.",
      thresher: "When the little talon floats over your head, dear, you take a long, tidy walk, well away from everyone, and step aside when the ring has burned down. He runs exactly where you walked, and the longer the walk, the smaller his landing. Everyone else keeps off your prints.",
      bloodreaver: "The sign was not mine, which offended me. So I kept off the walker's prints and stood my ground. Walk far and he lands light; stand still and he falls on you like a house. I would never stand still. Wounds should be chosen.",
      runeguard: "The sign is a pale talon with a ring that burns down. He will run the very steps of whoever bears it and land where they stood at the end; so the bearer walks one long line away from us and steps aside as the ring ends, and the rest of us keep off it.",
      gorgemaw: "My host serves the hunt to whoever wears the little talon, a dish of footprints eaten first to last. So the wearer walks a long line, far from the table, and leaves the plate when the ring is gone. The rest of us do not nibble at their prints.",
      oathbroker: "The clause: whoever bears the talon is hunted along the exact steps they have walked, and the longer those steps, the lesser the penalty at their end. So the bearer walks a long line, clear of my co-signatories, and steps aside as the ring expires. We keep off their prints.",
      ironpalm: "He lowered his head at one of us, and a ring began to burn down over them. That one walked, far and straight, away from us. He ran their steps and landed on the last one, and it was a small landing, because the walk was long. They had already stepped aside.",
      ranger: "Talon over a head. That one walks a long line, away from the rest, and steps off the end when the ring burns down. He runs their tracks and lands at the end; long track, small landing. A standing target gets flattened. The rest of us stayed off their prints.",
      shadowblade: "He looked at one of us, and the sign appeared. If it is mine, I do what I always do: slip away along one long, quiet line, far from the others, and step out of it just as the ring dies. If it is theirs, I stay off their prints. He is only reading.",
      pyromancer: "A talon appeared over my head and the ring around it burned down. I am not allowed to stand and shine, and must walk one enormously long line away from everyone, then step aside as it ends! The longer the walk, the smaller his pounce. I resented it, and obeyed.",
      thermist: "A ring burned down over my head. Orders, clipped: long line, away from the others, step aside at the end. Then the rush: he landed small where I had stood, because the walk was long. Short walks get a wide landing. Everyone else, off my prints.",
      driftblade: "A talon over my head and I was off, a long line across the ice, as far from everyone as the lake allowed, and sideways off the end of it the instant the ring ran out, because he runs exactly where you ran and lands where you stopped, small if you ran far.",
      alembist: "Cause: he reads the footprints of whoever wears the talon and runs them, end to end. Cure: the patient walks one long line away from everyone, and steps aside as the ring burns out; the longer the line, the smaller the landing. Everyone else keeps off the prints.",
      threadweaver: "One of my stitches got the talon and had to go, a long straight seam across the ice away from the rest of us, then a sidestep as the ring burned down. He runs the seam and lands on its last stitch. A short seam, and he lands wide. We kept off the thread.",
      marionettist: "A spotlight in the shape of a talon found one of the company, and their exit was a long one: far from the rest, one straight line, then a step aside as the ring burned out. Our star runs exactly where they walked. The company stays off their footprints.",
      cleric: "A talon appeared over one of my friends. I told them gently to walk one long line away from us and to step aside as the ring burned down, for he runs the very steps and lands where they stop. A long walk means a light landing. The rest of us kept off their prints.",
      grovekeeper: "The talon showed over one of my lot, and I knew what to plant: a long line, far from the rest, stepped out of when the ring ran down. He hunts the footprints like a dog after a hare and lands where they end; walk far and he lands small. We stayed off the furrow.",
      chronomancer: "The talon appears, and the ring burns down, and we all know how it ends. I walked a long line away from everyone, stepped aside on the last beat, and he landed on the empty end of it. Short walk, wide landing; long walk, small. Everyone else stayed off my prints.",
      tidecaller: "A talon rose over one of us like a fin. That one walked a long, slow line away from the rest and stepped aside as the ring ebbed. He runs their prints exactly and lands where they stop; the further they went, the smaller the splash. The rest of us stayed off their wake.",
      lanternkeeper: "I had the talon over my own head this time. One long line away from the three others, and off its end when the ring was a stub. He lands where the line ends, small if it was long. Three lamps stayed off my prints.",
      kenseier: "He lowers his head at one of us, and a ring burns down: that is the count. The one with the talon walks long, away from the others, and steps aside before the last beat. A long walk makes a small landing. The rest of us leave their prints alone.",
      houndmaster: "Pale talon over one of us. He'll run every step that one walked and drop where they ended, like a hound on a poacher's line. So the walker goes long and straight, away from the rest, and steps off the end. Stand still and he falls on you. Dogs stayed off the prints.",
    } },
    'Throat Snap': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "His jaws came for me alone, a wedge along the line between us. I waited until the head dropped, shield up in that last instant, and the others kept out of the wedge behind me. One bite, taken whole.",
      gloomsworn: "The jaws were meant for me and I let them find me. I drew the dark in at the last moment and took the bite whole. The rest of the party kept out of the wedge in front of him. It hurt, and then it was over.",
      thresher: "His jaws go straight for our shield, dear, in a wide wedge, so everybody else stays out of the wedge, nice and tidy, and the shield braces for it. The healer should have something ready afterwards, because it is a very large bite.",
      bloodreaver: "He snapped at me, a wedge of jaws from his face to mine, and I let it land. Beautiful. Everyone else stayed out of the wedge. I answered the bite after, with interest.",
      runeguard: "He turns his head toward the shield, and the wedge of jaws follows the line between them; the sign is plain. The ward goes up a breath before, and the rest of us stay out of the wedge. The healer's work comes after, for it is a deep bite.",
      gorgemaw: "A single bite served to me, straight from my host's mouth to mine, in a wedge. I opened wide and took it on the chin while the other guests kept out of the wedge. Rich, heavy, and quickly gone.",
      oathbroker: "The bite is owed by whoever stands before him, and the wedge of it is drawn on the ice in advance. I accepted it in full with a defence set beforehand, and my co-signatories stayed out of the wedge. The healer settles the balance afterward.",
      ironpalm: "He spoke with his jaws, to me alone, in a wedge. I did not raise my arms until its last instant, and took the word. The rest stood outside the wedge. The healer answered afterwards.",
      ranger: "Jaws on the shield, a wedge from his mouth along the line to the knight. Everyone else off that line. Shield took it with a defence up. Healer topped him after. Easy to read from the flank.",
      shadowblade: "He bit at our shield, which is what a mark does when it is being polite. The wedge of jaws follows the line to him, so I stayed out of it, off to the side, and the shield braced. It is only the healer's evening that gets longer.",
      pyromancer: "A wedge of jaws lit up from his face toward the knight, and I got out of it with my spell half cast! The knight braced, the healer waited until it landed, and then she worked. I finished from the side.",
      thermist: "Wedge of jaws, from his face to the shield. Not a step into it. The shield braced and took it, and the healer mended after. I stood too near once and felt the frost of it, and ran cold for a long while.",
      driftblade: "The jaws came for the shield in a wedge, and I ran wide of it, round and behind, never near the line between him and the knight, while the knight braced and the healer waited until the bite had landed before she mended.",
      alembist: "Cause: one heavy bite, aimed along the line from him to our shield. Cure: everyone else out of the wedge; the shield has a defence ready before it lands; the strong dose goes in afterwards, not before. Note: the bite is the loudest thing he does.",
      threadweaver: "He snapped at our shield, a wedge of jaws along the line between them, and I kept my other stitches out of it. The shield braced, I mended after the bite and not before, and the thread held.",
      marionettist: "The star bit at our knight, a wedge along the line between them, and the company stayed out of it. The knight braced, and the healer waited and mended after. (My Doll watched from the wings, safely behind the wedge.)",
      cleric: "The jaws went for our knight along a wedge, and the rest of us kept out of it. I laid the light on him before the bite and mended what it took after. He staggered, and stayed up.",
      grovekeeper: "The jaws came for my big fellow in a wedge, and my lot stayed out of it. I'd planted on him before, so the mending was already growing when it landed, and I tended the rest after. A short, hard frost.",
      chronomancer: "The bite is aimed at our shield, a wedge along the line from him, and it lands as his head comes down. Everyone else stayed out of the wedge, the shield braced, and I will have mended him already; I did.",
      tidecaller: "A bite like a breaking wave, aimed at our shield along the line from him. The rest of us stayed out of the wedge, and the shield braced, and I raised the water beforehand and eased it after.",
      lanternkeeper: "The jaws came down the line to our shield, a wedge drawn on the ice, so I stayed out of it with the other lanterns. I hung a light on the shield before the bite and kept it burning after. One light, one shield.",
      kenseier: "His jaws follow the line to the shield, and they come after a long breath. I drew the ash about me and met them as they landed. The others stayed out of the wedge. A direct blow, and honest.",
      houndmaster: "Big lad snapped at the shield-man along a wedge. Dogs and the rest of us kept out of it, flanks only. Shield-man braced and took it. Healer mended after. Like standing wide of a bull's horns.",
    } },
    'Howl': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "A howl, up and out across the whole lake. No shield turns a sound. The line stood close to the healer and took it already mended. It cost us all the same.",
      gloomsworn: "The howl came over all of us at once and found every one of us hollow. I have felt colder. We stood close by the healer, were mended before it, and mended again after.",
      thresher: "He tips back his great head and howls, dear, and it reaches every one of us. Nowhere to step, nothing to dodge; just be mended beforehand and stay near the healer. It is only a song. A cold one.",
      bloodreaver: "The whole lake rang with it, and every one of us took it. Nothing to dodge, nothing to hit back at, and for once I hated that! I stood near the healer, topped up beforehand, and took it standing.",
      runeguard: "The sign is the lifting of his head. The howl reaches all, and no ward turns a sound; we gather near the healer, whole before it lands, and are mended again after. It is the least subtle thing he does.",
      gorgemaw: "A howl, which is only a sauce poured over the whole room. No plate escapes it. We sat close to the healer, filled up beforehand, and took it together. Thin, and over quickly.",
      oathbroker: "The howl is a levy on the whole party, payable at once, and there is no clause to avoid it. We stood close by the healer, covered in advance, and paid it jointly. A small instalment, in the scheme of the evening.",
      ironpalm: "He lifted his head and spoke to all of us at once. There was no answer to give but to stand close to the healer, whole beforehand, and listen. Then he was silent, and we went on.",
      ranger: "Howl. Reaches everyone, no lane out of it. Closed up by the healer, topped off before. Took it standing. Nothing to shoot, nothing to hide behind.",
      shadowblade: "Even I cannot hide from a sound. He howled, the whole lake took it, and I stood near the healer, mended beforehand, and waited it out. He aims it at nobody and hits everyone.",
      pyromancer: "He howled and every one of us took it. Nowhere to stand that was safe, so I stopped sulking about my spell, stood close to the healer, and let her mend me before and after. It rang in my teeth for ages.",
      thermist: "He howls, and the whole lake goes brittle. No dodging it. Stay near the healer, mended beforehand. I shivered through the noise and thawed after. Too loud, that one.",
      driftblade: "He howled and there was no running from it, which is the worst kind of danger there is, so I ran in close to the healer anyway, since that was the only thing left to run to, and took it with her light already on me.",
      alembist: "Cause: a howl, whole party, no aiming. Cure: everyone gathered near me with a dose in them beforehand, and another after. Note: nothing to dodge, so nothing else to do. It is the one thing he does that I can bottle.",
      threadweaver: "The howl ran along every thread at once. I drew my stitches in close and mended before it landed, so nobody was left slack when it came. It hurt a little everywhere, which is the way sound hurts.",
      marionettist: "A howl from the star, straight at the back wall of the house, and it reached the whole company. There is no blocking for a noise. We gathered near the healer, mended in advance, and took the note together. (The Doll covered her ears.)",
      cleric: "He howled across the whole lake, and it came over every one of us. I called my friends close and laid the light on them beforehand, and they came through it whole. It frightened me more than it hurt them.",
      grovekeeper: "The howl came over us like a cold front, no dodging it. I'd planted on every one of my lot before, and we stood close, and it blew over. Weather turns, and so does a howl.",
      chronomancer: "The howl reaches everyone, the moment his head tips back. We gathered close, I had mended each of us already, and it was over before the echo came back off the far shore. I will have been ready; I was.",
      tidecaller: "A howl rolled out over the ice like a swell and broke on all of us together. I had raised the water high before it came, and we stood close, and it spent itself on a full shore.",
      lanternkeeper: "He howled, and it reached all four of us. I lit each of us beforehand, one lamp apiece, and we stood close so none burned low. It was a long note, and it passed.",
      kenseier: "A howl has no beat to meet and no edge to turn. A blade cannot cut it. We stood in close beside the healer, mended beforehand, and outlasted it together.",
      houndmaster: "Big lad howled and it hit the lot of us, dogs too. Whistled the pair in tight by the healer, got mended before it landed. No outrunning a noise. Rust whined a bit and Ash didn't.",
    } },
    'Rime': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "A red ring followed two of the others, a breath of frost. They went out to the edges, apart from the line, and the rime fell where they stood. Slick mist stayed behind, so we kept off it and my footing stayed good.",
      gloomsworn: "A red ring followed two of us, and the frost came down where each one stood. I was not chosen. They walked apart from the rest and left a slick patch behind, and I stayed off it. Slow ground kills men too, in its way.",
      thresher: "Two of us get a red ring that follows them about, dear, so those two wander off to the edge, away from everybody, and the frost lands there. It leaves a slick, slow patch of mist behind, so do not drop it where we will need to run.",
      bloodreaver: "A red ring chased two of my friends and burst where they stood! They took it off to the edge, away from everyone, and the frost left slick mist that slows whoever wades in. I stepped around it. I hate being slowed.",
      runeguard: "A red ring follows each of two heroes, and the breath of frost falls where it ends; those two go apart from us all. The slick mist it leaves slows any who tread it, so it is dropped where no one will need to run, and we pass around it.",
      gorgemaw: "Two guests had a red plate follow them, and the frost came down on it. Those two went off by themselves, to the far corners, so the sauce did not splash the rest of us. A slick, slowing glaze stays where it lands. Do not serve it on the road.",
      oathbroker: "The frost is owed by two named parties, each followed by a red circle, and neither may stand near the others when it falls. A slowing mist remains where each lands; it is to be deposited where no co-signatory needs to walk.",
      ironpalm: "Two rings of red followed two of us. They went apart, to the quiet edges, and the frost fell on empty ice. Where it landed, a slick mist stayed, and it slowed anyone who stepped in. We walked around it.",
      ranger: "Red ring on two of us. Those two peel off to the edges, away from the rest, and the frost lands clear. Leaves slick mist that slows whoever crosses. Dropped it off the lanes. Nobody wants to cross it running.",
      shadowblade: "A red ring chose two of us, and I was one, which I took as a compliment. I drifted to a far corner, away from everyone, and let the frost fall on nothing. The slick mist it left was somewhere no one needed to run.",
      pyromancer: "A red ring followed me about, and I was NOT impressed by the compliment! I took it off to the edge, away from everyone, and the frost fell on empty ice. Where it landed, a slick, slow mist stayed. I picked a corner nobody would run through.",
      thermist: "Red ring on me, following. Away from the others, out to the edge, and let it fall there. Where it fell, the ice went slick with mist and slowed anyone wading through. Drop it somewhere nobody needs to run. I ran cold, and spent the frost.",
      driftblade: "A red ring on me and I ran it out to the far edge, round the lot of them, as far as the ice would let me, and let the frost fall on nothing, and the slick mist that stayed was somewhere I would never want to run anyway, so I didn't.",
      alembist: "Cause: frost follows two of us, in a red circle, then breaks. Cure: those two go well apart from everyone, and drop it on bare ice where nobody will run. Residue: a slick mist that slows. Label: do not step in the residue.",
      threadweaver: "Two of my stitches had a red ring on them, so I cut their threads loose and sent them to the edges; the frost fell on bare ice. The mist it left was slick underfoot, and we sewed our routes around it.",
      marionettist: "Two of the company were followed by a red ring, so those two took their cues to the far edges, apart, and the frost fell on empty boards. The slick mist it leaves slows a runner. (A prop to be set where nobody has to exit.)",
      cleric: "Two of my friends had a red ring follow them. I sent them out to the edges, apart from everyone, so the frost fell on empty ice. The mist it left is slick, and slows whoever treads in it. I asked them to drop it where nobody would need to run.",
      grovekeeper: "A red ring on two of my lot, like a frost warning, and the sensible thing is to send them out to the far edges, away from the rest. Where it fell, a slick, slow mist stayed. Nobody needs to run through the edge of the field.",
      chronomancer: "Two of us wear the ring, and the frost falls where each stood as it breaks. They went out to the edges and far from one another, and the slick mist stayed there, where nobody needed to run. It slows a runner, and I have been that runner.",
      tidecaller: "A red ring followed two of us like a rip following a swimmer. They went out to the shallows, far from everyone, and the frost fell on empty ice. The mist stayed on it, slick and slow. We kept it off the paths.",
      lanternkeeper: "Two of us had a red ring on them. I picked a lamp up and set it down at the edge, away from the others, and those two stood by it while the frost fell. Slick mist stayed behind, slow underfoot, so it lay somewhere out of everybody's road.",
      kenseier: "Frost with a count to it: two rings of red, two walkers, and the beat falls when they stop. They went to the far edges before it did. The patch it left was slick, and we went around.",
      houndmaster: "Red ring on two of us, dogs and all. Walked mine out to the wall, away from the rest, and the frost fell on bare ice. Left a slick patch that slows a runner. Put it where nobody'd run. Rust'd have found the slow spot first.",
    } },
    'Mist-Hounds': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "Two of the others got an eye over their heads, and a hound each began to walk their steps. They ran straight at one another and through, and the hounds met behind them and went out. I kept my place and left them the ground to cross.",
      gloomsworn: "A hound of mist walked in the very prints of whoever had the eye over their head. It cannot be killed, and it bites if it catches you. The two followed ran at each other and through, and the hounds met and went out. I stood aside for it.",
      thresher: "Two of us get a little eye overhead, dear, and a hound of mist walks their very footprints, a touch slower than we are. Standing still gets you bitten, so the two of them run straight at one another, past, and the hounds meet and go out.",
      bloodreaver: "Mist-dogs on two of my friends! Each walked their own prints after them and bit when it caught up, and you cannot hit them, which I resented. So the pair ran straight at each other and through, and the dogs met and vanished. Neat.",
      runeguard: "An eye over a head: a hound of mist will walk that hero's own steps, slower than they, and bite if it closes. It cannot be struck. Two followed heroes run at each other and through, so their hounds meet and cancel. Later there are three, and one is left over.",
      gorgemaw: "My host sent two little hounds of mist after two of us, each following its guest's footprints, plate to plate. They cannot be eaten, and bite when they catch you. The cure is for the pair to run at each other and through, so the hounds meet in the middle and are gone.",
      oathbroker: "Each of two named parties is followed by a hound of mist along the very steps they walked. The hounds cannot be struck and bite when they overtake. The remedy: the two parties run at one another and through, whereupon the hounds meet and extinguish.",
      ironpalm: "A hound of mist walked behind me in my own footprints, a little slower than I walk. It cannot be struck. The other followed ran at me, and I at them, and we passed, and the hounds met behind us and were gone.",
      ranger: "Eye over two of us. Mist hound on each, walking the exact steps we left, a hair slower. Can't be shot. Stand still and it bites. The two run straight at each other and through; the hounds meet behind and go out.",
      shadowblade: "A hound of mist took my scent, rude of it, and followed my own steps. A blade does nothing to mist, so I did what a gentleman does: ran straight past the other hunted one, and let the two hounds find each other and vanish.",
      pyromancer: "An eye hung over my head and a hound of mist began walking my footprints! You cannot burn mist. I ran straight at the other followed one and through, the hounds met behind us, and both went out. Standing still would have meant a bite.",
      thermist: "Eye overhead, and a hound of mist on my prints, slower than me but patient. Don't stand still. The other followed one and I ran straight at each other and through, and their hounds met and went out. I ran hot the whole way.",
      driftblade: "A hound walked my footprints and I ran straight at the other hunted one and through, round and past, and our two hounds met behind us and went out together, and neither bit, because I never stopped for long enough for a bite.",
      alembist: "Cause: a mist hound on two of us, walking our footprints, a little slower than we do. Cure: run the two of us straight at each other and past, so the hounds meet and cancel. Note: they cannot be killed, and they bite if you stand still.",
      threadweaver: "A hound of mist trailed each of two of us along our own seam, just behind. They cannot be cut. The two of us ran straight at each other and past, and the hounds met at the crossing and went out. Standing still gets you bitten.",
      marionettist: "Two of the company wore an eye overhead, and a hound of mist walked each one's own blocking, a little behind. Cross the stage straight at your partner and through, and the hounds meet in the middle and exit. (Stand still, and it bites.)",
      cleric: "A hound of mist walked behind two of my friends, in their own footprints, and bit them if they stopped. I told them to run straight at each other and past, so the hounds would meet and go out. It cannot be harmed, poor thing.",
      grovekeeper: "A mist hound followed each of two of my lot along their own tracks, like a dog after a plough. Nobody can hit it. Those two ran straight at each other and through, the hounds met behind, and both faded. Standing still just feeds the bite.",
      chronomancer: "The hound follows my steps a little slower than I take them, so it always arrives where I have been. The two followed ran straight at each other and through, and their hounds met and went out. I would have it done twice by now.",
      tidecaller: "Two hounds of mist crept after two of us on our own footprints, slowly, like water filling a footprint in sand. They bite if you stop. We ran at each other and through, and the hounds met and dissolved. There was no killing them, only meeting them.",
      lanternkeeper: "A small hound of mist followed each of the two with an eye over their head, walking their prints, just slower than they walk. Two walkers, two hounds: I sent them straight at each other and past, and the hounds met and went out. Standing still gets you bitten.",
      kenseier: "A hound of mist walks my steps and keeps a slower beat than mine. A blade cuts nothing in it. The two followed ran toward each other and through, and their hounds met and went out. When there are three, one is walked until it fades.",
      houndmaster: "Mist hounds on two of us, each on its own man's prints. Can't be hurt, bite if you stand still. The pair ran straight at one another and through, hounds met behind and went out. Rust sulked about it. Odd one out, walk it until it fades.",
    } },
    'Circle the Prey': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "He trotted a ring round the party, and the ring was drawn as he went, a band of prints. Inside it was safe and outside it was safe; the band was not. I picked my ground inside it, the line in close, and the band burst empty.",
      gloomsworn: "He circled us at a trot and left a ring of prints, and the ring burst. The band is the only bad ground; the middle and the far side are both fine. I stayed in the middle. Nobody likes being surrounded, but the dead are used to it.",
      thresher: "He trots all the way round us, dear, and the ring he leaves bursts: a red band. Inside it is quite safe, and so is outside, so simply don't stand on the band. I like the middle myself.",
      bloodreaver: "The big one circled us and the band of his prints burst, a ring with a hole in the middle! I could have stood in the band and taken it, but it was a fool's wound. The middle was safe, and I stood there.",
      runeguard: "He trots a ring round the party along his own track, and the track bursts. The ring is a red band; within it is safe, and beyond it is safe. The sign is the prints appearing ahead of him as he goes, so choose the middle or the outside before he closes it.",
      gorgemaw: "My host walked a ring round our table and the ring went off like a pie dish: a red band, with a clear middle and a clear rim. I stayed in the middle with the other guests, and only the crust burned.",
      oathbroker: "The Hound circles the party and the band of his track is charged, as a levy: inside it is exempt and outside it is exempt, and the band itself is not. I remained within, with my co-signatories, and the clause did not reach us.",
      ironpalm: "He walked a ring around us and the ring spoke, a red band. In the middle, nothing. Outside it, nothing. Only the band was loud. We stood in the middle and listened to it burst.",
      ranger: "Wolf circles the party at a trot, leaving a ring of prints. Ring bursts. Middle's safe, outside's safe, the band isn't. Held the middle with the rest. Watched the circle close round us.",
      shadowblade: "The mark circled us, painting a band of his own prints on the snow, and it burst. Neither the inside nor the outside minded. I stood in the middle, where he was not looking, and the band went off around us like applause.",
      pyromancer: "He trotted a ring round us and the band of his prints went up in red, the most dramatic frame I have ever stood in! Safe inside, safe outside; the band was the only trouble. I took the middle, in the spotlight, and finished my spell.",
      thermist: "He circles us, and the ring of prints bursts: a hot red band. The inside is cool. The outside is cool. Don't stand in the band. I stood in the middle and watched it flare around us.",
      driftblade: "He trotted round us in a ring and the prints burst behind him in a band, and I stayed where I was, which I hate, in the middle, because the middle and the outside were both safe and only the band was not, and I wasn't going to run into it.",
      alembist: "Cause: he circles the party and his track bursts as a ring-shaped band. Cure: be inside it, or outside it. The band is the only hazard. Note: the middle is easier to stay in, and easier to heal.",
      threadweaver: "He circled us and the track he left burst in a ring, a red band. I drew my stitches into the middle and we waited. Inside was whole and outside was whole; only the band was hot. A neat round seam, and nothing to unpick.",
      marionettist: "Our star walks a full circle round the company, and his path bursts: a red band, the whole way round. The middle is safe, and so are the wings beyond. Keep off the band. (An entrance in the round. The company stayed centre stage.)",
      cleric: "He trotted a ring round us, and his prints burst in a red band. Inside it was safe, and outside it was safe. I called everyone into the middle and held them there, and the band went off around us, harmless.",
      grovekeeper: "The old wolf circled us like a fence going up, and the fence burst. Inside was safe and outside was safe; the fence itself was not. My lot stayed in the middle and let the band go off round them, like a hedge fire.",
      chronomancer: "He circles us, and the track he leaves bursts all at once on his last step. The band is the only danger: stay inside it or outside it. We stayed in the middle. I think we did last time as well.",
      tidecaller: "He circled us the way the sea closes round a rock, and the ring he left burst. The middle was dry, and the far side was dry, and only the band was wet. We stayed in the middle and the water went round us.",
      lanternkeeper: "He trotted a ring round all of us, and I watched his prints light up ahead of him. The band burst once he closed it. Inside was safe, so I set our lamps there and kept us together. Outside was safe too.",
      kenseier: "He walks a ring round us, and the count is the length of the circle. His track bursts; the band is the stroke. Inside it is clear and outside it is clear. We stood in the middle and let the stroke fall on empty ice.",
      houndmaster: "Big lad trotted a ring round us like a dog round a flock. Prints burst in a red band. Middle's safe, outside's safe, and the band isn't. Dogs stayed in the middle with me. Easy once you've watched a collie do it.",
    } },
    'Snowdrift': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "A flurry swept the whole lake and the snow took every print, every patch of mist and every ridge of ice. Nothing to block and nothing to dodge. The ground was fresh again. I stood easy, and the line came together.",
      gloomsworn: "Snow swept the lake and wiped it clean: our prints, the mist, the ice, the little hounds. A man would give a great deal for a wipe like that. It is only snow, and there was nothing to do but stand in it.",
      thresher: "A great flurry sweeps the lake, dear, and every footprint is gone, and the slick mist, and the walls of ice, and the little hounds. Nothing to dodge. It is a lovely moment to take a breath, and a clean sheet to start on.",
      bloodreaver: "Snow came sweeping across the lake and took away everything: the prints, the mist, the ice, the hounds. No wound in it, no fight in it! It was the one thing I could not hit. We took the breath and waited.",
      runeguard: "The flurry is read plainly: it erases every footprint, the mist, the ridges and the lesser hounds, and hurts no one. The page is clean again. Whatever is walked after it is new ink, and the next hunt begins from nothing.",
      gorgemaw: "A flurry cleared the cloth: footprints, mist, ice walls and the little hounds, all wiped away as if by a napkin. Nothing hurt, nothing eaten. A palate cleanser, and I took it gladly.",
      oathbroker: "A flurry strikes the lake, and the record is expunged: every footprint, every patch of mist, every ridge, every lesser hound. No claim is made upon the party. All accounts begin afresh, the next hunt's included, so walk accordingly.",
      ironpalm: "The snow swept across the lake like a long exhale. Every print was gone, and the mist, and the ice, and the hounds. No word to answer. We stood on fresh ground and let the room go quiet.",
      ranger: "Flurry crosses the lake. Wipes every print, mist, ice ridge, mist hound. No lane to read. Fresh snow, no tracks. Good time to move to a better stand. Next hunt starts from nothing.",
      shadowblade: "The snow wiped the lake clean, every print of mine and everyone's, the mist, the ridges, the hounds. I would have liked to leave nothing of myself behind all night, so I was quietly grateful. Nothing aimed at anyone; nothing to avoid.",
      pyromancer: "A flurry blew across the lake and put out every footprint, every slick of mist and every wall of ice, and the little hounds! Not a thing left to burn. For a moment the stage was blank, and I was most offended.",
      thermist: "Snow sweeps across the whole lake. Every print gone, the mist, the ridges, the hounds. Nothing to dodge, so I stopped. Blank white. A clean sheet, and a cold one. Anything walked after it is new.",
      driftblade: "A flurry blew across the lake and wiped the lot, every print I had run across it, the ridges, the mist, and the hounds, and nothing to dodge, and I stood still for it with the most dreadful patience, because the white was clean and it was mine.",
      alembist: "Cause: a flurry across the lake. Effect: it wipes footprints, mist patches, ice ridges and every lesser hound. Cure: none needed. Note: the page is clean, and a hunt that starts now has no trail to read.",
      threadweaver: "The flurry swept the lake and unpicked everything: prints, mist, ridges, hounds. No harm in it, only a clean cloth. I used the quiet to tie my stitches together again. Anything walked afterwards is new thread, so walk it on purpose.",
      marionettist: "A flurry sweeps the stage and strikes the set: every footprint, the mist, the ice walls, the hounds. No cue to dodge. A blank stage, and a blank script for the next scene. (The Doll caught a flake on her tongue.)",
      cleric: "A flurry went across the lake, and it did no harm; it only took away the prints, the mist, the ridges of ice and the little hounds. We had a moment to breathe, and I mended whoever needed it. Everything after was new.",
      grovekeeper: "The snow came across the lake like a blanket over a field, and under it every print and every patch of mist and every wall of ice was gone, and the hounds too. Nothing to dodge. A clean field, ready to be walked.",
      chronomancer: "A flurry crossed the lake and undid every footprint, every patch of mist and every ridge, and the hounds, which is my own trick done better. It aims at no one. What anyone walks after it is new.",
      tidecaller: "A squall of snow crossed the lake and smoothed it as the sea smooths sand: prints, mist, ridges, hounds, all gone. No harm in it, and nothing to mend. A good moment to rest in, and the next hunt starts from smooth ground.",
      lanternkeeper: "A flurry swept over the lake, and all the footprints went out like lamps in a gale: the mist, the ridges of ice, the little hounds too. No harm in it. I topped up our wicks in the quiet and set us down on clean snow.",
      kenseier: "A flurry crosses the lake with no beat and no edge. Every footprint is wiped, with the mist, the ice and the lesser hounds. There is nothing to meet. We rested in it, and began again from nothing.",
      houndmaster: "Snow came across the lake and wiped the lot: prints, mist, ice, mist hounds. Dogs shook it off. No harm in it. Good time to get a drink and look round. Anything you walk now is new.",
    } },
    'The Long Chase': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "After the flurry he vanished into the white and hunted three of the others one after the other. Each started with no prints at all and had to run at once, a long line, and step off its end. The last of it was a howl, which the line took together.",
      gloomsworn: "He vanished into the white and hunted three of us in turn. The snow had been wiped, so each hunted one had no trail and had to run the instant the talon showed, far and straight, then step aside. A howl closed it. A long night for the runners.",
      thresher: "He slips away into the whiteout, dear, and hunts three of us, one after another. Everyone begins with no prints at all, so the moment the talon appears over you, run, run a long line, and step aside at the end. A howl closes the dance.",
      bloodreaver: "He vanished into the white and came for three of my friends, one at a time, and each had no trail, so each ran at once, as far as the ice allowed, then sidestepped the landing! A howl followed. I wished one had been mine.",
      runeguard: "From the white he hunts three in turn. The snow has been wiped, so each begins from no trail at all; the one with the talon runs at once, writes a long line before the ring ends, and steps off its end. The sequence closes with a howl.",
      gorgemaw: "Three courses, served one after another out of the white, each to a different guest, each on a blank plate: no prints at all. The guest runs at once, as far as possible, and steps aside from the last bite. A howl is the dessert.",
      oathbroker: "Three parties are hunted in sequence from the white, each beginning with an empty record. The party bearing the talon must run at once and run far, writing the line before the ring expires, then step off its end. A howl concludes the proceedings.",
      ironpalm: "He went into the white and spoke three times, each to a different one of us. Each of us began with no prints and had to run long and far, then step aside. After the third, a howl, and we gathered and took it.",
      ranger: "Wolf's gone in the whiteout, hunts three of us one after another. Each starts with no tracks. Run the moment the sign shows, long line, step off the end. Ends with a howl. Held my ground till my turn.",
      shadowblade: "He melted into the white, which I admired, and hunted three of us in turn. Nobody had a trail, so when the talon came to me I ran at once, long and quiet, and stepped off the end. A howl to close, because he cannot leave without a curtain call.",
      pyromancer: "The whole lake went white and he vanished and chose three of us, one after the other! Nobody had any prints at all, so the moment the talon appeared I ran like a thing on fire, a long line, then a step aside. A howl to finish.",
      thermist: "Whiteout. He hunts three of us one after the other, and there are no prints to read. When the talon showed over me: sprint, long line, then sidestep at the end. A howl at the close. I ran hot the whole way, and it was the right temperature for it.",
      driftblade: "He vanished into the white and hunted three of us one after the other, each from clean snow with no trail, so when the talon came to me I ran at once, as far as the ice would let me, and stepped off the end, and then the howl came.",
      alembist: "Cause: he vanishes and hunts three of us in turn, each starting with no footprints at all. Cure: run at once when the talon shows, a long line, then a step aside. Close: a howl, so keep a dose back for it.",
      threadweaver: "He slipped into the white and hunted three of us, one after another, each starting with a blank cloth, no thread laid at all. So I ran the moment the talon showed, long and straight, and stepped off the end. We closed up for the howl after.",
      marionettist: "A blank white stage and three solos: the star hunts three of the company, one after another, each with no footprints laid. When the talon finds you, exit at a run, a long line, and step aside at the end. The finale is a howl. (The Doll sat this act out.)",
      cleric: "He vanished into the white and hunted three of my friends in turn, and each began without a single print behind them. I begged each to run at once, as far as they could, and step aside at the end. A howl closed it, and I had the light ready.",
      grovekeeper: "He slipped into the snow like a wolf into a hedge and hunted three of my lot, one after the other, each on a bare field with no tracks. Each ran at once, a long furrow, and stepped aside. The last was a howl, and I had planted for it.",
      chronomancer: "He hunts three of us, one after another, from a wiped lake. There is no trail to read, so when the talon came I ran at once and ran long, and stepped off at the end. The howl at the close came on the last beat, as it always does.",
      tidecaller: "Out of the white he came for three of us, one after another, each with no footprints on the snow. When the talon rose over me I did not wait. I ran a long line across the ice and stepped aside, and the howl rolled in after.",
      lanternkeeper: "Three hunts in a row out of the white. No one had prints to start with, so whoever had the talon ran at once, one long line, and off its end. Then a howl. Three runs, one howl, and four lamps still lit.",
      kenseier: "He vanishes into the white and hunts three of us in turn. Each begins with nothing behind them and must run at once and run far, then step aside from the landing. The sequence ends in a howl; a blade cannot turn it, so we gathered and outlasted it.",
      houndmaster: "Big lad went into the white and hunted three of us in turn, each from fresh snow, no prints to start with. Run the second the sign shows, long line, off the end. Howl at the finish. Dogs ran with me through it.",
    } },
    'The Whole Trail': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "The snow stopped, and the prints stayed. Now the hunt runs a long stretch of the hunted one's steps, everything they have walked, not just the last of it. So the one with the talon keeps their walk short and straight, and the rest of the line stays off it.",
      gloomsworn: "The snow stopped falling, and the prints stopped fading. The hound runs every step the hunted one has walked in a long while, so it matters where I have been. I walked less. A man who stands at his post leaves a short trail.",
      thresher: "Ah, the snow has stopped, dear, and the prints stay, and he runs a great long stretch of the walker's steps now, not only the last few. So don't wander before the sign, and walk one straight line after it, and everyone else stay off the old tracks too.",
      bloodreaver: "The snow stopped and our prints stayed on it, every wandering one, and now the hunt runs the whole of the hunted one's walk! I had been running about for the love of it. Next time, a straight road, and let my friends keep off every part of the old line.",
      runeguard: "The snow has ceased, and the prints no longer fade. The hunt now runs a far longer stretch of the bearer's steps, all that they have walked. Walk little, walk straight, and remember where you have been; the rest of us stay off all of it.",
      gorgemaw: "The snow stopped, and the leftovers stayed on the floor. Now the hunt runs a long, long stretch of one guest's footprints, everything they walked, in a course of many turns. Walk less, walk straight, and do not forget where the plate has been.",
      oathbroker: "The snow has ceased and the record no longer lapses: the whole walk of the hunted party is now entered, not only its latest stretch. The party must walk little and straight, and all others stay clear of every part of the old line.",
      ironpalm: "The snow stopped, and the prints stayed. Now he runs the whole of a long walk, not the last of it. Walk little, walk straight. I kept my own steps few, and the others stayed off the old tracks.",
      ranger: "Snow's stopped. Tracks don't fade now, and the hunt runs a long stretch of them, all that the hunted one walked. Walk less. Walk straight. Remember every place you stood. Everyone else off the old tracks.",
      shadowblade: "The snow stopped, and the prints stayed, which is a great inconvenience to anyone who likes to come and go quietly. I walked as little and as straight as I could, and the others kept off my old tracks.",
      pyromancer: "The snow stopped falling and our prints stayed, every silly wandering one, and now the hunt runs a huge stretch of the walker's steps! I had been pacing about like a diva. Walk less, walk straight, and everyone off the old tracks.",
      thermist: "Snow stopped. Prints stay. The hunt now reads all of a walker's wandering, not the last bit. Walk less, walk straight. I had paced all evening, and ran hot at the thought of it.",
      driftblade: "The snow stopped and the prints stayed, every loop and turn I had ever made, and the hunt runs the whole of it now, so I made one long, straight line instead of a hundred small ones, and everyone kept off my old circles.",
      alembist: "Cause: snow stopped, prints stay, and the hunt runs a much longer stretch of one walker's steps. Cure: fewer steps, in a straight line, and know where you have been. The rest stay off the old prints, all of them.",
      threadweaver: "The snow stopped, so my stitches' prints stayed, and the hunt ran a far longer length of thread than before. Walk less, walk straight, and keep the old seams clear of the rest. A tangle of prints is a tangle of trouble.",
      marionettist: "The snow stopped and the prints stayed, as if the stage were never swept. The hunt runs a long, long stretch of the hunted one's blocking now. Cross the stage as little as you can, and straight; everyone else stays off the old chalk.",
      cleric: "The snow stopped, and the prints did not fade any more, so the hunt ran a much longer stretch of my friend's steps. I begged them to walk less and walk straight, and the rest of us kept off every old track.",
      grovekeeper: "The snow stopped, and the field held every track, and the hunt now runs the whole of the walker's furrow. Walk less, walk straight, and remember where you have been. The rest of us keep off the old rows.",
      chronomancer: "The snow stops and the past stops fading, so the hunt now runs a long stretch of my own steps, as much as I have walked. I walked less, in a line, and knew where I had been. The rest stayed off the old tracks.",
      tidecaller: "The snow stopped falling, and the prints stayed, like tracks on a beach with no tide to wash them. The hunt runs a long stretch of them now. Walk little, walk straight, and keep from the old line.",
      lanternkeeper: "The snow stopped, and the prints did not fade. The hunt runs all that a person has walked in a long while, so I kept my own walking short and straight, and we kept off one another's old steps. I knew where my lamps had been.",
      kenseier: "The snow ceases, and the prints do not fade; the hunt runs the whole of a long walk. A short, straight form is best. The hunted one walked less, and the rest of us stayed off every step they had already taken.",
      houndmaster: "Snow's stopped. Prints stay. Hunt runs a long stretch of one man's steps, the whole walk, not the last of it. Walk less, walk straight. Dogs and I kept off the old tracks. Rust still wanted to follow them.",
    } },
    'Cold Trail': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "The trail went cold, and he stopped waiting. No shield turns that cast. We had spent too long dodging and too little hitting. Next time, every opening used, and the hound down before the moon was high.",
      gloomsworn: "He ran out of patience, and the cast that ended us went through the whole party. I have waited longer than that on a post. We were too slow, and we had walked too many wasted lines. Down him sooner.",
      thresher: "The hour came for us instead, dear: he stopped waiting, and the whole lake went white. We had been too slow, and too fussy over the footprints. Next time, hit him in every pause, and be done before the last cast.",
      bloodreaver: "The trail went cold and he ended all of us in one breath, and I did not even get to bleed for it! We were too slow, my friends. Hit him harder, hit him more, and finish him before he runs out of patience.",
      runeguard: "The last cast was foretold in the margin: when the long hunt ends, he stops waiting. We had not finished. A faster party strikes in every pause and loses less time to the footprints. Be done before it falls.",
      gorgemaw: "The last course arrived and I was not done with the second. The Hound put out every lamp on the lake, and the table was cleared with all of us still at it. A dawdling party is a poor guest. Eat faster.",
      oathbroker: "The term expired, and the Hound ceased waiting; the final cast fell upon all. Our performance fell short and our time was spent poorly in dodging what we might have struck. Next time, strike in every opening, and settle early.",
      ironpalm: "The trail went cold, and he stopped answering and simply ended us. We had been slow, in the dodging and in the striking. Next time, fewer wasted steps, a harder strike in every pause, and the hound down before he speaks.",
      ranger: "Trail went cold. Cast landed on the whole party. Too slow: too many careful steps, not enough shots. Hit him whenever he's out of the mist. Finish before the last cast. No time left.",
      shadowblade: "The trail went cold and the lake closed over every one of us. Not even I could slip out of that. We had been too polite about the footprints. Strike whenever he is out of the mist, and have him finished before the cast ends.",
      pyromancer: "The trail went cold, the cast came, and every one of us went white at once! My spells were too few and too shy, and I wasted a good many on modesty. Burn harder, burn longer, and finish him before the cast begins.",
      thermist: "Trail gone cold. The cast reached the whole lake and every one of us at once. Slower than the frost. Too many steps wasted on the footprints. Hit hard in every opening and finish early. I ran cold, and then I ran out.",
      driftblade: "The trail went cold and the cast came, and I ran, of course I ran, and there was nowhere to run to, because we had spent too long dodging and not long enough striking. Next time, fewer laps and harder hits.",
      alembist: "Cause: the long hunt runs out, and so does his patience; the final cast reaches everyone. Cure: finish earlier. Note: our dose was too slow, and the openings were not spent in full. Label: this batch spoils.",
      threadweaver: "The trail went cold and the cast cut every thread at once. We had been too slow, and too much time was spent unpicking our own footprints. Next time, hit him harder in every opening and finish early.",
      marionettist: "The trail went cold, the house lights went out, and the curtain came down on all of us. We had played the footprints too carefully and the finale too late. Strike in every pause, and be done before the last cast. (The critics were unkind.)",
      cleric: "The trail went cold and the last cast reached every one of us. I had been mending too much and urging too little. Next time I will tell them to strike harder, and to finish him early.",
      grovekeeper: "The trail went cold like a late frost, and the last cast took the whole garden at once. We had dithered over our footprints, and the season ran out. Next time, strike hard in every opening and be done before the frost.",
      chronomancer: "The trail went cold at the same hour, however often I undid it. We had been too slow, too careful, and too many of our openings went half used. Next time we hit harder and finish before the cast.",
      tidecaller: "The last cast came in as a flood that never turns, and every one of us went out with it. The water had risen while we lingered over footprints. Next time, quicker, and harder in every gap.",
      lanternkeeper: "The trail went cold and every lamp on the lake went out at once. We had spent too many wicks on careful footprints and too few on striking. Next time, hit him in every opening and have him down while the oil lasts.",
      kenseier: "The trail went cold, and he stopped waiting. It is a cast with no beat to meet and no edge to turn. We arrived at the end behind schedule. We spent our strokes on caution, and he spent his on us.",
      houndmaster: "Trail went cold and the whole lot of us went down with it. Took too long on the dodging, not enough on the biting. Next time, hit hard whenever he's in the open, and have him finished first.",
    } },
    'Double Scent': { hard: true, learned: {
      bastion: "Two of the others carried the talon, one with a small number one over it and one with a two. He ran the first's steps and landed, then the second's. Neither line crossed the other, and I kept off both. A crossing would have sent him to whoever stood at its end.",
      gloomsworn: "Two of us bore the talon, a one and a two. He runs one's steps, then two's. Each walked far from the other so their lines never crossed, for where his road crosses a fresher track he turns onto it. I stayed off every line.",
      thresher: "Two of us get the talon with a little number each, dear: first one, then the other. Both take a long walk, away from everyone and away from each other. Don't let the lines cross: where they do, he simply turns onto whichever is fresher and runs it.",
      bloodreaver: "Two talons, a one and a two, and he took them in order! Each walked their own long line, well apart. Where a line crosses a fresher track he turns onto it and runs it to the person at the end, so I kept off all of it.",
      runeguard: "Two bear the sign, numbered. He runs the first line, lands, then the second; so the two walk long lines, well apart, and neither crosses the other, for where his road meets a fresher one he turns onto it and follows it to whoever stands at its end.",
      gorgemaw: "Two plates, numbered: one dish and then the next. The two guests walked far, each their own way, and never crossed. Where his road meets a fresher track he turns onto it and runs it to whoever stands there. I kept off the numbered ones' paths.",
      oathbroker: "Two parties are named in sequence, numbered one and two, and he hunts them in that order. Their lines must not cross: where his road meets a fresher one, he turns onto it and runs it to whoever stands at its end. Each stepped aside as the ring expired.",
      ironpalm: "A one and a two over two heads. He took the first road, then the second. Each walker went far from the other and crossed nothing, for he turns onto any fresher track he meets. I stood off both roads.",
      ranger: "Two on the sign, numbered one and two. Hunted in that order. Each walked a long line, away from the rest and from the other. Lines mustn't cross: he turns onto the fresher one at a crossing and runs it to whoever's standing there. Kept off both.",
      shadowblade: "Two talons, a one and a two, taken in turn, which is only good manners. I walked mine well away from the other, because where his road meets a fresher one he turns onto it and runs to whoever stands at its end. Neither line crossed, and nobody stood on them.",
      pyromancer: "Two talons, a one and a two, and he took them in turn! We each ran a long line away from everyone and from each other, because where he meets a fresher track he turns onto it. Mine did not cross anyone's. I resented being second.",
      thermist: "Two talons, a one and a two. Long lines, far apart, never crossing: where he meets a fresher track he turns onto it and runs it to whoever stands at its end. Cold logic, then the rush: one landing, then the other, and everyone else off both lines.",
      driftblade: "Two talons, a one and a two, and he took them in order, so the two of us ran long lines opposite ways across the ice and never crossed, because he turns onto any fresher track he meets, and I would not be the one he found at its end.",
      alembist: "Cause: two marked, numbered, taken in order. Cure: each walker draws one long line, far from the other, and steps aside at its end. A fresher track turns him onto it, so no line may cross. Bystanders keep off both.",
      threadweaver: "Two of my stitches carried a numbered talon. He ran the first seam and landed, then the second. They sewed their lines far apart and never crossed, for where his road meets a fresher thread he turns onto it. The rest of us stayed off both.",
      marionettist: "Two soloists, a one and a two, each given a talon: he runs the first's route, then the second's. Exit in opposite directions, long lines, never crossing; where his path meets a fresher one he turns onto it. The company keeps off both.",
      cleric: "Two friends bore the talon, numbered, and he hunted them in order. I asked them to walk wide of one another, never crossing, since a fresher track turns him. The rest of us stayed off both lines, and I mended after.",
      grovekeeper: "Two furrows ploughed for him, numbered, run in turn. Each of my two walked long and far from the other, because he turns onto any fresher track he meets. They didn't cross. The rest of my lot stayed out of both rows.",
      chronomancer: "Two talons, a one and a two, run in that order, one landing and then the second. The two lines must never cross: a fresher track turns him onto it. We walked them apart, and I will have known which was first; I did.",
      tidecaller: "Two talons rose, a one and a two, and he ran them in turn like two long waves. The pair walked far from each other, and their lines never crossed, for he turns onto the fresher wake. The rest of us stayed off both.",
      lanternkeeper: "Two had a talon, numbered one and two. I lit nothing near either line and kept the others off both. They walked long lines, far apart, never crossing, for where his road meets a fresher one he turns onto it. One landing, then the next.",
      kenseier: "Two bear the talon, one and two, and he hunts them in order, one stroke and then the next. Their lines are long, far apart, and never cross; he turns onto any fresher track he meets. Everyone else keeps from both lines.",
      houndmaster: "Two on the sign, a one and a two, hunted in that order. They went long and far apart, no crossing, because he'll turn onto any fresher track and run it to whoever's standing there. Dogs and I stayed off both lines.",
    } },
    'Doubling Back': { hard: true, learned: {
      bastion: "He ran my comrade's line to its end, turned, and came back down it to the start. Two landings. The walker stepped off the end and stayed off. I held my footing well clear of both places, and the line stood clear of the road.",
      gloomsworn: "He runs the walked line to its end, then turns around and runs it again, back to where it began. I stayed off the line and clear of the starting place, and clear of any fresher track that might turn him. A road can be walked twice.",
      thresher: "He runs the whole line, dear, and then, quite rudely, runs it again the other way. So step off the end and do not step back onto the line once he has passed. The place where the line began gets a pounce as well, so stay clear of it too.",
      bloodreaver: "He ran the line, landed, then turned and ran it BACK! A wicked trick. I stayed off the whole line, and off the place it began, where he lands a second time. The hunted one stepped aside and stayed aside.",
      runeguard: "The hunt is run twice: out along the walked line, then back along it to where it began, with a second landing there. The hunted one steps off the end and does not return to the line; the rest of us stay off it and clear of its beginning.",
      gorgemaw: "A second helping of the same dish: he runs the line to its end, lands, and runs it all the way back, landing again at the start. Step off the end and stay off the line. Do not sit near where it began.",
      oathbroker: "The clause is invoked twice: he runs the line to its end and lands, then returns along it to the beginning and lands again. The hunted party steps aside and does not re-enter the line; all others stay clear of the line and its first step.",
      ironpalm: "He ran the road, and then ran it again, back to where it began. Two words from one stroke. I stepped off the end, and kept off the road afterward. The place where it began gets its own landing, so we stood away from that too.",
      ranger: "He runs the line out, lands, runs it back, lands again at the start. Step off the end and stay off the line after he passes. Keep clear of the start too, and of any fresher track he could turn onto. Two pounces to one track.",
      shadowblade: "The mark ran the line out and then back again, which is greedy. I stepped off the end, and stayed off the line. The place where it began got a second landing, so I stood well away from that too. Not once did he look at me.",
      pyromancer: "He ran the line, landed, and ran it back to where it began, and landed again! I stepped off the end and was NOT tempted to stand on my old road. Nobody stood where it began, either. Two landings for one walk, which is showy.",
      thermist: "Out along the line, a landing, then back along it, a second landing at the start. Step off the end, and stay off the line after. Keep away from the beginning too. I stood too near the old road once and felt it scald.",
      driftblade: "He ran my line out and ran it back, and landed twice, once at the end where I had stepped off and once at the start where I had begun, so I ran wide of both and never touched the road again.",
      alembist: "Cause: he runs the walked line, lands at its end, then runs it back and lands at its start. Cure: the hunted one steps aside at the end and never returns to the line. Everyone else keeps off the line and its first step. Dose twice.",
      threadweaver: "He ran my seam out, then back, and landed at both ends. I stepped off the far end and did not return to the thread, and the rest of my stitches stayed off it and off the place where it began.",
      marionettist: "A double exit: out along the walked line to a landing, then back along it to the start, and another. The hunted one steps off the end and stays off the line. The company keeps clear of the line and of where it began.",
      cleric: "He ran the line and landed, then ran back along it and landed where it had begun. I called everyone away from the line and from the place it started. The hunted one stepped aside and stayed aside, and I mended after.",
      grovekeeper: "He ploughed the line out and ploughed it back, landing at both ends. The hunted one stepped off the end and stayed off the furrow, and my lot kept clear of it and of the place where it began. Two harvests from one row.",
      chronomancer: "He runs the line, lands, and then runs it again backwards, to the place where it began. We stayed off the line and clear of the start. The hunted one stepped aside once, and stayed there.",
      tidecaller: "The line went out like a wave leaving and came back like a wave returning, and he landed at both ends. The hunted one stepped off the end and stayed off; we kept clear of the road and of its first step.",
      lanternkeeper: "He ran the line, landed, then came back along it and landed at the start. I set our lamps off the line and well away from where it began, and the hunted one stepped aside at the end and did not go back to the road.",
      kenseier: "The first stroke runs the line out and lands; the second runs it back and lands at the start. The hunted one steps off the end and stays off the line. The rest of us keep clear of the line and of its first step.",
      houndmaster: "Big lad ran the line out, landed, then ran it back to the start and landed again. Stepped off the end and stayed off the road. The rest kept clear of the line and where it began. Out and back, like a good retriever.",
    } },
    'Buried Scent': { hard: true, learned: {
      bastion: "The talon went up, and halfway through the cast a flurry swept the snow clean. So the hunted one waited for it to clear and then ran, hard and straight, for all they were worth. A line from before the flurry was worth nothing. The rest of us kept off the new one.",
      gloomsworn: "A flurry came in the middle of the cast and wiped every print, including the long walk the hunted one had already made. I watched them stand and wait for the white to clear, and then run. A short road meant a wide landing. I kept off it.",
      thresher: "The talon goes up, dear, and then a flurry sweeps the lake in the middle of it and wipes all your tidy footprints away. So don't dawdle in the white: the very moment it clears, run as far and as straight as you can, and everyone else stays off that new line.",
      bloodreaver: "The flurry came down in the middle of the hunt and took every print I had made! Fine, I thought, I would write a new one. The moment it cleared I ran, hard and straight. A short, wasted walk would have meant being fallen on. We kept off the new line.",
      runeguard: "A flurry falls midway through the cast and erases the walked line; only what is walked after the snow clears is taken. The hunted one waits for it to clear, then runs at once, far and straight. The rest of us stay off that fresh line, and the old one.",
      gorgemaw: "My host wiped the plate in the middle of the course: a flurry, and every footprint was gone. The hunted guest waited for the snow to clear, then ran as far as the ice allowed, for the old walk no longer counted. We kept off the new line.",
      oathbroker: "A flurry intervenes mid-term and expunges the record, so that only what is walked after the snow clears may be taken against the hunted party. That party must therefore sprint the moment it clears. Every other party keeps off the new line.",
      ironpalm: "The flurry spoke in the middle of the hunt and swept our prints away. The hunted one waited for the white to settle, and then ran, as long and straight as breath would let them. The old walk counted for nothing. We stayed off the new one.",
      ranger: "Flurry mid-cast wipes the lake. Old tracks gone, so only what's walked after counts. Hunted one stands, waits for it to clear, then sprints a long straight line. No point walking before. Rest of us stay off the new tracks.",
      shadowblade: "A flurry fell in the middle of the hunt and wiped my careful footprints away, which was rude. I waited for it to clear, then ran a long, straight line the instant I could see. The old tracks counted for nothing, and the others kept clear of the new.",
      pyromancer: "The flurry hit in the middle of the cast and wiped out my whole beautiful walk! I made myself stand there in the white, and the instant it cleared I ran, a long straight line, as fast as my legs allowed. The others kept off it.",
      thermist: "Talon up, then a flurry mid-cast: every print gone. Stand and wait for it. The instant it cleared: sprint, a long line, no turning. Everything before the flurry was wasted. The rest kept off the new line.",
      driftblade: "A flurry fell in the middle of the hunt and wiped my whole run away, so I waited, which I hate, until the snow cleared, and then ran, flat out, a line straight across the ice as far as I could get, because only that last stretch counted.",
      alembist: "Cause: the flurry lands mid-cast and wipes every print. Effect: only the walk after it counts. Cure: the hunted one waits for it to clear and sprints at once, long and straight. Everyone else keeps off that new line.",
      threadweaver: "A flurry swept the lake in the middle of the cast and unpicked my whole seam. Only what I stitched after it counted, so I waited for the white to clear, and then ran a long straight line. The others stayed off the new thread.",
      marionettist: "Mid-scene, a flurry sweeps the stage and wipes the blocking away. Only what is walked after counts, so the hunted one waits in the white and exits at a run the moment it clears, long and straight. The company keeps off the fresh line.",
      cleric: "The flurry came in the middle of the cast and wiped away all they had walked. I told my friend to wait for it to clear, and then to run at once, as far and as straight as they could. The rest of us stayed off the new line.",
      grovekeeper: "A flurry blew through the middle of the hunt and swept the field bare. The hunted one waited for it to clear, then ploughed one long straight furrow, as fast as they could go. Only that counted. The rest of my lot kept off the new row.",
      chronomancer: "The flurry comes in the middle of the cast and takes all the steps before it. Only the steps after count. So the hunted one waited for it to clear and ran at once, far and straight. I had seen it, so I stayed off the new line.",
      tidecaller: "A squall crossed the lake in the middle of the hunt and smoothed the snow flat. Only the new tracks counted, so the hunted one waited until the squall cleared, then ran as far and straight as the ice allowed. We stayed off that wake.",
      lanternkeeper: "The flurry came halfway through the cast and put out every footprint. The hunted one waited for the white to clear and then ran, one long line, no pause. The old walk counted for nothing. I kept our lamps off the new line.",
      kenseier: "The snow falls hard mid-cast, and every old step is gone; the count begins again. The hunted one holds until it thins, then cuts a straight line the length of the lake. We left that line alone.",
      houndmaster: "Snow came down mid-hunt and wiped the lot. Only the last stretch counts, so the hunted one waited for it to clear and ran, long and straight. Old walk was worth nothing. We kept off the new line, and the dogs with us.",
    } },
    'The Second Hound': { hard: false, extreme: true, learned: {
      bastion: "A second talon, violet-grey, and a shade of him ran its steps while he ran the first. Two hunts at once: both walkers went long and apart, and the line kept off both. A red dot over a head means red prints, and he takes those first. I let nothing avoidable touch me.",
      gloomsworn: "Two hunts at once: the hound's own, and a shade's on a violet-grey talon. Both walkers went long, on separate roads. A hero hurt by what could have been avoided leaves red prints and steers the hound. I was not hurt. I have never before been proud of that.",
      thresher: "A second talon, dear, in violet-grey, and a shade who hunts it as he hunts the first. Everyone walks a long road of their own, well apart. And take no hit you could have stepped out of; the red prints it leaves are what he follows.",
      bloodreaver: "A second talon, violet-grey, and a ghost of the big one hunting alongside him! I stood by while both walkers made their long roads, far apart. Hits I could have dodged leave red prints, and he chases red over anything. I took only the hits I chose.",
      runeguard: "The sign is a violet-grey talon: a shade hunts a second hero as the Hound hunts the first. Two lines, long and apart; two landings together. The ledger has a new margin as well: a hero struck by something avoidable leaves red steps, and at any crossing red is read first.",
      gorgemaw: "My host brought a pale second serving of himself, violet-grey on the plate, and both dishes landed together. The two guests walked their own long roads, apart. A spill on the cloth leaves a red stain, and he follows the stain. I ate neatly.",
      oathbroker: "Two hunts are now concurrent: the Hound's, and his shade's, under a violet-grey sign. Each party walks its own long line, clear of the other. Further, any party struck by an avoidable blow is entered in red, and red takes precedence over all other tracks at a crossing.",
      ironpalm: "He spoke twice at once, once aloud and once in a pale shade. Two walkers, long roads, well apart. A blow I could have stepped away from leaves red prints, and red is what he hears first. I kept my steps clean.",
      ranger: "Shade of the wolf, second talon in violet-grey, hunts at the same moment. Two tracks, long, no overlap. Anyone hit by something avoidable leaves red prints, and red wins at a crossing. Stay unhurt, stay off both trails.",
      shadowblade: "The mark brought a shade, a violet-grey talon on another of us, and both hunts ran at once, which is showy. Two long walks, kept apart. I take no avoidable hits; those leave red prints, and he follows red like a bloodhound.",
      pyromancer: "Two hunts at once, the second a violet-grey talon on a ghost-hound's account! Two long runs, apart. I refused every avoidable hit, because those leave red prints, and he will always follow red. I did not go red; that is for sunsets.",
      thermist: "Second talon, violet-grey, a shade hunting alongside the hound. Two long lines, never touching. Careless hits leave red prints, and red steers him at a crossing. Keep clean. I ran cold, and spotless, and both pounces fell on empty ice.",
      driftblade: "A shade took a second scent and the two hunts ran together, so the other walker and I ran long and opposite ways, and I took no hit I could dodge, because a hit leaves red prints and red is the track he takes at any crossing.",
      alembist: "Cause: a shade hunts a second hero, marked violet-grey, in step with the Hound. Cure: two long lines, apart. Side effect: avoidable hits leave red prints, and red takes precedence at a crossing. Keep the record spotless.",
      threadweaver: "A shade took up a second thread of ours, violet-grey, and both hunts ran together along two long seams, well apart. A stitch caught by something avoidable leaves a red thread, and he always follows red. I tied nobody to a mistake.",
      marionettist: "A pale understudy, the shade, takes a second part under a violet-grey talon, and both hunts play at once. Two leads, long routes, apart. Any avoidable hit leaves red prints, and at a crossing he follows the red. No missed cues.",
      cleric: "A violet-grey talon on another of my friends, and a pale shade hunted them at the same moment as the hound hunted the first. I asked both to go far and apart. And I scolded anyone hit by something avoidable, kindly, since red prints draw him.",
      grovekeeper: "Two furrows ploughed at once, one by the wolf and one by his shade on a violet-grey talon, and kept well apart. No avoidable hits in my garden: they leave red tracks, and he follows red before any other at a crossing.",
      chronomancer: "Two hunts on one beat, the shade's under a violet-grey talon: two long lines, apart. A hero hit by something avoidable leaves red prints, which he takes at a crossing. Next time I will have dodged it; this time I did.",
      tidecaller: "A shade like his reflection on black water took a second scent, a violet-grey sign, and both ran together. Two long, wide-set lines. Anyone struck by what they could have avoided leaves red prints, and he follows red when roads cross.",
      lanternkeeper: "Two talons at once, one pale and one violet-grey, and two long lines from two hunts, kept apart. Anyone hurt by something avoidable leaves red prints, and he follows red first, so I kept my lamps clear of everything that hurt.",
      kenseier: "He keeps two counts now: the Hound's, and a shade's on a violet-grey talon. Each walker walks long, away from the other. An avoidable blow taken leaves red steps, and red is read first at a crossing. A mistake leaves a line to follow.",
      houndmaster: "Second talon, violet-grey: a shadow-dog hunting alongside the big lad. Two long lines, well apart, and both landings together. Anyone caught by something they should've dodged leaves red prints, and he'll follow red first. Stayed unhurt.",
    } },
    'Stillness': { hard: false, extreme: true, learned: {
      bastion: "Two of the others wore a small square block overhead and stood rooted, and the walker had to write a long line past neither of them. I kept my ground off its road. When the blocks went out and the trail lit, I watched the two step clear.",
      gloomsworn: "Two heads carried a stone block and did not move; a step under it would burst red. The one with the talon walked a long road that missed both and ended far from them. I stood off it. Standing still is a trade I already knew.",
      thresher: "I wore the little square block, dear, so I stood like a lamp-post, and the walker took their long line well past me and finished far away. When the block went out and the line lit up, I stepped aside, as one does.",
      bloodreaver: "Two of my friends were told not to move, or their steps would burst red! I wanted the walker to cut a line close past them, for the thrill, but it ran wide of both and ended far off. The still ones stepped off when the blocks faded.",
      runeguard: "A small square sign marks two heroes who must not move; a step taken beneath it is struck red as the trail locks. The scented hero's line must therefore pass neither and end far from both. When the signs lapse, the two step clear of the lit line.",
      gorgemaw: "Two guests had a lid set over their heads and could not stir a step. The one with the talon laid a long line that skirted both plates and ended at the far side of the room. Once the lids lifted, the pair moved off the line.",
      oathbroker: "Two parties are bound not to move, on pain of a red charge for every step. The party with the talon must therefore lay its long line clear of both and end far from them, since they cannot give way. When the bond lapses, they step clear if need be.",
      ironpalm: "Two of us were told to be still. The walker's line had to pass neither and end far from both, for they could not step aside. When the sign went, and the trail lit, they took a breath and stepped away from it.",
      ranger: "Talon on me, square blocks on two others who can't move. Long line, wide of both, ending far from either. They can't dodge for me. Once the signs drop and the trail lights, they step off it if it's near.",
      shadowblade: "I wore the square block, so I stood perfectly still, which is my best trick, and let the walker draw a long line past me at a decent distance. When the sign dropped and the line lit, I slipped aside. Not a step wasted.",
      pyromancer: "A square block over my head and the order was STAND STILL, the worst order there is! Every step would burst red behind me, so I stood and fumed while the walker ran far past. The instant the sign went, I got well clear of the lit line.",
      thermist: "Square block over me. Frozen: no steps, or they burst red. The walker laid a long line wide of me and finished far off. When the sign went out and the trail lit, I thawed fast and stepped away. Too close is still too close.",
      driftblade: "Two others were nailed in place by square blocks and I had the talon, so I ran a long, wide curve around both of them and out to the far end of the lake, as far from them as the ice allowed, then sidestepped the landing.",
      alembist: "Cause: two bystanders under square blocks cannot move. Cure for the hunted: a long line that passes neither and ends far from both. Cure for them: once the sign drops and the trail lights, step aside if the line or the landing is near.",
      threadweaver: "I had the talon, and two of my stitches stood pinned under square blocks, unable to step out of my way. So I sewed my long seam wide of both and finished far from them. When the blocks went out, they stepped clear of the lit thread.",
      marionettist: "A square block over my head: hold the pose, do not move a foot. The walker's long route went past me, well to one side. The moment the block was gone and the trail lit, I made my exit. (The Doll had been very good.)",
      cleric: "I had the square block, and I was not to take a single step, or it would burst red. I trusted my friend with the talon to walk far wide of me, and they did. When the sign faded I moved clear of the lit line at once.",
      grovekeeper: "Told to take root, I took root, with a square block over me and not one step to spare. The walker ploughed a long furrow wide of me and ended far off. Once the block was gone and the trail lit, I stepped out of its way.",
      chronomancer: "The sign was a square block over two heads, and it meant do not move. So whoever had the talon walked a long line wide of both and ended far off, since they could not step aside. I will have stepped clear once it lifted; I did.",
      tidecaller: "The talon was mine, and two others stood stuck under square blocks like rocks in a stream. I bent my long line round both, wide, as the current goes round stones, and out to the far shore. When the blocks went out they eased clear of the lit trail.",
      lanternkeeper: "Two of us stood fixed under square blocks, like lamp-posts. I had the talon, so I walked a long line that passed neither and ended far from them, and they stepped clear once the blocks went out.",
      kenseier: "Two bear a square sign and have no beat to move on; a step taken under it is struck in red at the lock. The one with the talon walks wide of both and ends far away. When the signs go, the two step off the line.",
      houndmaster: "Square block over me, so I stood like a post and so did the dogs. Hunted one walked a long line wide of me and ended far off. Block went out as the trail lit, and I stepped off sharp. The dogs sat. Fine.",
    } },
  },
};
